      "caseNotes": "NORTHGATE SURGERY — REFERRAL\nPatient: Lucas Bramley\nAge: 18 months\nReferred to: Dr Eleanor Vasquez, Consultant Paediatrician, Children's Outpatient Department, Northgate Hospital\nSeen: 4 September 2026\n\nReason for referral:\nFaltering growth with loose stools and strongly positive coeliac serology. Assessment and confirmation requested.\n\nGrowth:\nWeight has fallen from the 50th centile at 12 months to the 9th centile today. Length remains on the 50th centile. Head circumference on the 50th centile. Weights: 9.8 kg at 12 months, 9.9 kg at 15 months, 10.1 kg today.\nWeight at 6 months: 7.9 kg, on the 50th centile.\nParent-held record brought to the appointment; all weights plotted today.\nLength 82 cm today. Head circumference 47.5 cm.\n\nSymptoms:\nFrequent loose, pale and offensive stools, three to four a day, for around four months. Irritability, particularly in the afternoons. Reduced appetite, noted by his mother since he was weaned onto cereals at around ten months. Abdominal distension.\nNo vomiting. No blood in the stools.\nMother keeps a stool diary, which shows no clear link to milk or any single food.\nMild nappy rash during the worst weeks, now settled.\nTeething; molars coming through.\nStools were formed and once daily before these symptoms began.\nNo cough or recurrent chest infections.\nSleeps through most nights, waking once.\n\nExamination:\nThin, with reduced subcutaneous fat over the buttocks. Mildly distended abdomen, soft, no organomegaly, no masses. Alert and interactive throughout. No rash. No mouth ulcers.\nTemperature 36.9. Well hydrated. Capillary refill under two seconds.\nNo oedema. No clubbing. Chest clear; heart sounds normal.\n\nDevelopment:\nMeeting milestones. Walking independently since 14 months. Six words. Normal hearing screen.\nPoints to show interest, follows simple instructions and feeds himself with a spoon.\n\nFeeding:\nVaried family diet, includes bread, pasta and breakfast cereal. Cow's milk 400 ml daily. No food refusal beyond normal for age.\nTypical day: cereal or toast at breakfast, sandwiches or pasta at lunch, family meal in the evening.\nEats gluten every day.\nDrinks from an open cup; bottle stopped at 12 months.\nOne small cup of diluted juice a day.\n\nInvestigations, 28 August 2026:\nMild iron-deficiency anaemia — haemoglobin 98 g/L, MCV 68, ferritin 8.\nAnti-tissue transglutaminase strongly positive. Total IgA normal.\nBlood test taken at the surgery; he tolerated it well.\n\nPast medical history:\nTerm delivery at 39 weeks, birth weight 3.4 kg. No neonatal concerns. Immunisations up to date.\nTongue-tie checked at birth; none found.\nViral gastroenteritis at 9 months, resolved within five days.\nOne ear infection at 11 months, settled within three days.\n\nCurrent medications:\nNone regular. Vitamin D drops daily.\nVitamin D drops started on health visitor advice in infancy.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nFamily history:\nMaternal aunt has coeliac disease.\nThe aunt is his mother's sister; she was diagnosed in her twenties and is well on a gluten-free diet.\nNo other family history of bowel disease, thyroid disease or diabetes.\n\nPlan:\nReferring for confirmation and dietetic input. The family has been advised specifically NOT to remove gluten from his diet before specialist review, as this would affect the results.\n\nSocial:\nLives with both parents and no siblings. No smokers in the household. Attends nursery two days a week.\nNursery staff report that he is sociable and naps well, but often leaves food at lunch.\nMother works part-time on the days he is not at nursery.\nFather has recently started a new job.\nGrandparents look after him one afternoon a week.\nFamily has a pet cat.\nHealth visitor last saw the family at his 12-month review; no concerns were raised then.\nFamily is planning a holiday to Cornwall in October.\n\nAlso mentioned today:\nMother asked about a nursery form. He is due his 18-month review.\nMother also asked which stage of car seat he should move to.\n\nParental understanding:\nMother has read about coeliac disease because of her sister and is anxious about the diagnosis.\nShe understands why gluten must stay in his diet until he has been seen, and has agreed to this.\nBoth parents would like to attend the appointment and have asked for a morning slot if possible.\nThey understand the specialist may suggest a further test before any change in diet.\nMother will keep the stool diary going until the appointment and bring it with her.\n\nOther:\nMother asked whether he can go to a friend's birthday party this weekend.\nInterpreter not needed.\nHe has a favourite toy rabbit that helps him settle for examinations.\nMother will bring his parent-held record to the clinic.",
      "recipient": "Dr Eleanor Vasquez, Consultant Paediatrician, Children's Outpatient Department, Northgate Hospital",
