      "caseNotes": "STONEBRIDGE SURGERY — TWO-WEEK-WAIT REFERRAL\nPatient: Mr Albert Finch\nAge: 66\nReferred to: Dr Wendy Acheampong, Consultant Respiratory Physician, Rapid-Access Lung Clinic, Stonebridge Hospital\nSeen: 4 September 2026\n\nReason for referral:\nSuspicious right upper lobe mass on chest X-ray with haemoptysis, hoarseness and weight loss. Urgent investigation requested.\n\nHistory:\nSix-week history of persistent cough, different in character from his usual COPD cough and not responding to two courses of antibiotics. Two episodes of small-volume haemoptysis, on 22 and 28 August 2026, described as streaks of blood in the sputum. Hoarse voice for three weeks, continuous, with no sore throat.\nUsual COPD cough: mornings only, small amounts of white sputum, unchanged for several years.\nBoth antibiotic courses were completed in full, with no improvement.\nNo chest pain. No difficulty swallowing.\nCough is worse at night and disturbs his sleep.\nNo wheeze beyond his usual.\nVoice is worse by the evening; his wife first noticed the change.\n\nConstitutional symptoms:\nUnintentional weight loss of five kilograms over two months, from 74 kg to 69 kg. Fatigue, sleeping in the afternoons, which is new. Appetite reduced. No night sweats. No bone pain. No headache.\nWeight had been stable at around 74 kg for several years before this.\nEating about half his usual portions. No fevers.\nStill managing his own personal care and short walks.\n\nExamination:\nNo clubbing. Reduced air entry in the right upper zone. No cervical or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy palpable. No superior vena caval obstruction. Chest expansion symmetrical.\nTemperature 36.7. Pulse 84, regular. Blood pressure 134/80.\nNot breathless at rest; speaking in full sentences.\nOral cavity and throat normal on inspection.\n\nInvestigations:\nChest X-ray 1 September 2026 — 3 cm mass in the right upper lobe, no effusion, no bony lesion reported.\nBloods 1 September 2026 — mild anaemia, haemoglobin 112 g/L. Corrected calcium normal at 2.38. Liver function normal. Sodium normal.\nRenal function normal; eGFR above 60.\nPrevious chest X-ray in 2019, during a chest infection: clear, with no mass.\n\nPast medical history:\nCOPD, diagnosed 2014. Ex-smoker, 40 pack-years, stopped four years ago. Occupational asbestos exposure as a welder between 1979 and 1994.\nBaseline: walks about a mile on the flat; breathlessness grade 2 on the MRC scale.\nSpirometry at his annual review, March 2026: FEV1 68 per cent of predicted.\nOxygen saturation 95 per cent on air at that review.\nOne exacerbation last winter, treated at home and fully resolved.\nLeft inguinal hernia repair 1997, uncomplicated.\n\nCurrent medications:\ntiotropium inhaler once daily\nsalbutamol inhaler as required\nInhaler technique checked today: good.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nFamily history:\nNothing relevant.\nHis brother has type 2 diabetes.\n\nPlan:\nReferring under the two-week-wait pathway. CT of the chest and abdomen arranged prior to clinic.\n\nSocial:\nLives with his wife. Retired welder. Drives.\nHis wife also drives and can bring him to all appointments.\nSpends most mornings on his allotment.\nInfluenza and pneumococcal vaccines up to date.\nBowel cancer screening kit returned last year: normal.\nDrinks about ten units of alcohol a week, mainly beer.\nTwo adult children, one living nearby.\nWorked at the same shipyard for most of his career; retired at 62.\nPlays dominoes at his local club on Friday evenings.\n\nAlso mentioned today:\nAsked about a repeat of his emollient. Reports long-standing hearing loss from occupational noise.\nWears bilateral hearing aids; prefers face-to-face conversation to the telephone.\nHis grandson is getting married next month; he asked whether hospital appointments might clash.\nAsked how much the hospital car park costs.\n\nExamination findings today:\nReduced air entry in the right upper zone. No clubbing. No palpable cervical or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy. Oxygen saturation 94 per cent on air at rest.\n\nSymptom timeline:\nCough changed in character approximately six weeks ago. Two episodes of blood-streaked sputum in the past fortnight. Weight down 5 kg over two months, unintentional.\n\nPatient understanding:\nHe has been told the X-ray shows a shadow that needs urgent tests, and that cancer is one possibility.\nHis wife was present. They wish to be kept fully informed and prefer appointment letters by post.\nHe has agreed to the CT scan and to the clinic appointment.\nHe prefers morning appointments where possible.\nHe would like his daughter to be copied into any appointment letters, as she helps organise his diary.\nHe asked whether he should keep using his inhalers as usual and was told yes.\nHe has never had a CT scan; he has been told what to expect, including the contrast injection.\nNo claustrophobia.",
