      "caseNotes": "FAIRMONT SURGERY — REFERRAL\nPatient: Mrs Anita Sharma\nAge: 45\nReferred to: Mr David Ashworth, Consultant General Surgeon, Upper GI Surgery, Fairmont Hospital\nSeen: 4 September 2026\n\nReason for referral:\nSymptomatic gallstones. Assessment for elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy requested.\n\nHistory:\nThree-month history of recurrent right upper quadrant colicky pain, coming on within an hour of a fatty meal and lasting from thirty minutes to several hours. Two more severe episodes, on 8 August and 27 August 2026, each lasting around four hours with nausea and vomiting. Pain radiates to the right shoulder blade. Between attacks she is entirely well.\nTypical triggers: takeaway meals, cheese and fried breakfasts.\nBoth severe attacks settled at home; she did not attend hospital on either occasion.\nHas already cut down on fatty foods, with fewer mild episodes over the past month.\nMild episodes once or twice a week, mostly in the evening after the main meal.\nPain up to 8 out of 10 during the two severe attacks.\nA heat pad during mild episodes gives partial relief.\nFirst episode in early June 2026, after a takeaway meal; she put it down to indigestion at the time.\n\nAbsent features:\nNo jaundice at any point. No fever or rigors. Stools and urine normal in colour. No weight loss. No change in bowel habit.\nWeight stable at 78 kg over the past year.\nNo itching. No heartburn.\n\nExamination today:\nAbdomen soft, mild right upper quadrant tenderness. Murphy's sign negative when settled. No palpable mass. No jaundice. Temperature 36.8. Pulse 76.\nNot clinically anaemic. Chest clear.\nBlood pressure 124/78. Oxygen saturation 99 per cent on air.\nHeight 1.64 m.\nBowel sounds normal.\n\nInvestigations:\nUltrasound 26 August 2026 — multiple gallstones, largest 12 mm. Gallbladder wall not thickened. Common bile duct not dilated at 4 mm. Liver and pancreas normal.\nBloods 26 August 2026 — liver function normal, amylase normal, full blood count normal, CRP 4.\nKidneys and spleen normal on the same scan.\nPrevious bloods in 2025, checked for tiredness: all normal.\nRenal function normal.\n\nPast medical history:\nNothing significant. Two previous uncomplicated pregnancies and vaginal deliveries.\nWisdom teeth removed under general anaesthetic in 2003; no anaesthetic problems.\nCervical screening up to date; last result normal.\n\nCurrent medications:\ncombined oral contraceptive pill\nibuprofen occasionally for headaches\nSame pill for eight years without problems.\nIbuprofen about twice a month.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\nNo sensitivity to plasters or dressings.\n\nLifestyle:\nBMI 29. Non-smoker. Alcohol four units a week.\nWalks with a local walking club on Sundays.\nDental check-up last month; no treatment needed.\nDiet before symptoms: frequent takeaway meals during night shifts.\nTwo cups of coffee a day.\n\nPlan:\nReferring for elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy. Advised a low-fat diet in the meantime and to attend urgently if she develops pain with fever, jaundice or persistent vomiting.\n\nSocial:\nWorks shifts as a nurse. Lives with her husband and two children.\nHusband works from home and can take time off to help during her recovery.\nHer daughter is starting secondary school this week.\nChildren aged 11 and 15.\nHer sister lives nearby and can help with school runs.\nWorks on a busy medical ward; usually three long day or night shifts a week.\nFamily has booked a half-term trip to see relatives in Leicester.\n\nAlso mentioned today:\nAsked about occupational health clearance after surgery. Reports occasional lower back pain.\nBack pain: mechanical, after lifting at work, improving with stretching exercises; no leg symptoms.\nAsked about car parking at Fairmont Hospital.\nHer employer requires a fitness-to-work note before she returns to clinical duties.\nImpact on work:\nHas taken four days off in the past three months because of the attacks, and finds night shifts particularly difficult as the pain has twice woken her during one.\n\nFamily history:\nMother had her gallbladder removed in her fifties, without complications.\n\nPatient understanding:\nShe understands that the operation is usually done by keyhole surgery and is keen to proceed.\nShe would prefer a date with a few weeks' notice so that she can arrange cover on the ward rota.\nShe knows when to seek urgent help and has been given the advice in writing.\n\nOther:\nAsked whether the operation could be done at the treatment centre nearer her home.\nShe would like to avoid the October half-term week, when the family is away.\nShe uses the practice app and would prefer appointment messages there.\nHer husband had a hernia repair at Fairmont Hospital last year and was pleased with his care.\nShe asked whether she should keep working night shifts until the operation; advised to be guided by her symptoms.",
