      "caseNotes": "BROOKHAVEN — TRANSFER SUMMARY\nPatient: Mr Gerald Thompson\nAge: 71\nTransferred to: Dr Ngozi Adeyemi, Consultant in Rehabilitation Medicine, Maple Ward, Brookhaven Rehabilitation Unit\nAdmitted: 23 August 2026 · Transferred: 4 September 2026\n\nReason for transfer:\nInpatient neurorehabilitation following a left middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke twelve days ago.\n\nPresentation:\nSudden-onset right-sided weakness and difficulty speaking, witnessed by his wife at 07:40 on 23 August. Right facial droop. Arrived within the thrombolysis window.\nAmbulance called at once; arrived at hospital at 08:35.\nBlood pressure 168/92 on arrival.\nCT head on arrival: no haemorrhage. CT angiogram: no large-vessel occlusion suitable for thrombectomy.\nBlood glucose normal on arrival.\n\nTreatment:\nThrombolysed at 09:25 on the day of admission. Repeat imaging at 24 hours showed no haemorrhagic transformation. Antiplatelet started at 24 hours.\nAdmission bloods: total cholesterol 6.1 mmol/L; HbA1c 39 mmol/mol (normal).\nBlood pressure on the ward settled at 130 to 145 systolic.\nCarotid Doppler: no significant stenosis.\nEchocardiogram: normal.\nNo bleeding complications after thrombolysis.\n\nCurrent status:\nRight arm power 3/5, right leg 4/5. Expressive dysphasia, improving — now using short sentences with occasional word-finding difficulty. Comprehension intact. Swallow assessed by speech and language therapy on 25 August and again on 1 September; safe for a normal diet and thin fluids.\nNil by mouth until the first swallow assessment.\nPressure areas intact.\nLow in mood on the third and fourth days; now brighter and engaging well with therapy.\nRight hand grip weak; can hold a cup with both hands.\nSitting balance good; standing balance improving.\nOccasional right shoulder ache, eased by positioning and paracetamol.\nSleeping well.\nWeight on the ward stable at around 82 kg.\n\nMobility:\nTransfers with the assistance of one. Walks short distances with a frame and one person. Has not attempted stairs.\nBefore the stroke he was fully independent, walking about two miles a day, and drove.\nUses a wheelchair for longer distances on the ward.\nNo falls during this admission.\n\nContinence:\nOccasional urinary urgency, continent with prompting. Bowels regular.\nUses a urinal bottle at night.\n\nCurrent medications:\nclopidogrel 75 mg daily\natorvastatin 80 mg at night\nramipril 2.5 mg daily\nparacetamol as required\n\nPast medical history:\nHypertension since 2012. Ex-smoker, stopped 2005. No previous stroke or transient ischaemic attack. Atrial fibrillation excluded on prolonged monitoring.\nLeft knee arthroscopy 1996.\nRight cataract surgery 2019, good result.\nBowel cancer screening up to date.\nBlood pressure was well controlled at his GP surgery before this admission.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nTherapy input to date:\nPhysiotherapy and speech and language therapy started on the ward, daily on weekdays. Occupational therapy assessment completed.\nPhysiotherapy has focused on standing balance and right arm function.\nSpeech and language therapy: word-finding practice with a communication book.\nOccupational therapy: needs help with buttons and shoelaces; washes his upper body independently.\nHe tires by mid-afternoon, so therapy has worked best in the mornings.\n\nPlan:\nOngoing multidisciplinary rehabilitation. Goals agreed with the patient: independent transfers, and discharge home.\nHis own longer-term goal is to walk to the newsagent at the end of his road.\n\nSocial:\nLives with his wife in a two-storey house with the only bathroom upstairs; the stairs are the main concern for discharge planning. Retired postman. Two adult children living locally.\nOccupational therapy home visit not yet arranged.\nHis wife visits daily and has joined some therapy sessions.\nHis daughter is expecting her first baby in December.\nKeen gardener; grows tomatoes in a greenhouse.\nHis son lives ten minutes away and has offered to help with any home adaptations.\nHis wife is in good health and keen to help with his care at home.\nHas a downstairs cloakroom with a basin but no toilet.\n\nAlso noted:\nAsked about driving. Wears reading glasses.\nAsked whether the rehabilitation ward has a television in each room.\n\nWard housekeeping:\nHis electric razor and radio travel with him in a labelled bag.\n\nPatient understanding:\nHe understands that he has had a stroke and that recovery will take time.\nHe is motivated and his main wish is to manage the stairs and be at home with his wife.\n\nOther:\nHis dog is being looked after by a neighbour.\nLikes to read the newspaper each morning.\nHe supports his local football club and listens to matches on the radio.\nHis wife has asked about visiting times on Maple Ward and about parking for visitors.\nDischarge summary and imaging reports sent electronically with this transfer.\nHe uses his reading glasses for the communication book.\nA routine hearing check is due at his GP surgery; no concerns at present.",
